# speckit.plan

## 1. High-Level Architecture
- **Pattern:** Class-based architecture.
- **Data Storage:** An in-memory Python list will be the single source of truth for storing task objects. No database or file persistence in this phase.
- **Core Components:**
  - A `Task` class to model individual to-do items (attributes: id, title, description, status).
  - A `TodoManager` class to encapsulate all business logic (CRUD operations) and manage the in-memory list of tasks.
  - A main execution script (`main.py`) to handle the console user interface (UI) and user input, delegating all actions to the `TodoManager`.

## 2. Class Definitions
### `Task` Class
- **Attributes:**
  - `id`: `int` (unique identifier)
  - `title`: `str`
  - `description`: `str`
  - `completed`: `bool` (True for "Done", False for "Pending")

### `TodoManager` Class
- **Attributes:**
  - `_tasks`: `list[Task]` (private list to store tasks)
  - `_next_id`: `int` (private counter to generate unique IDs)
- **Methods:**
  - `add_task(title, description) -> Task`
  - `get_all_tasks() -> list[Task]`
  - `find_task_by_id(task_id) -> Task | None`
  - `update_task(task_id, new_title, new_description) -> Task | None`
  - `delete_task(task_id) -> bool`
  - `toggle_task_completion(task_id) -> Task | None`

## 3. UI Flow (`main.py`)
- The application will run in a loop, presenting a menu of options to the user.
- The menu will include: "Add Task", "View All Tasks", "Update Task", "Delete Task", "Mark Task Complete", and "Exit".
- User input will be captured to determine which action to perform.
- The script will instantiate `TodoManager` and call its methods based on user commands.
